  "family": "Betulaceae",
  "memo": "Beaked hazelnut is a species of deciduous shrub native to most of North America, where it grows as an occasional component of the understory in mixed forests rather than as a dominant shrub. This species is notable for its distinctive fruit, an edible nut enclosed in a tubular, beak-like husk that gives the species its common name. It is occasionally cultivated in native plant gardens and naturalized landscapes, where it is valued for its benefits to wildlife and its hardiness. It should be grown in partial shade to full sun with moist, well-draining soil, and it is quite cold-hardy.",
